    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d442">

                <pb facs="0552" n="458" sameAs="frus:frus1930v03/pg458"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.20/349: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Liberia</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hall</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-07-02T11:00:00-00:44">July 2, 1930—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 2:50 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>92. George W. Lewis was commissioned yesterday afternoon<anchor n="34"
                        xml:id="d442-f1"/> as major in the Liberian frontier force. The President of
                    Liberia is of the opinion that Lewis is a mere military adviser who is outranked
                    by two Liberian majors and the Secretary of War here and gave instructions
                    accordingly.</p>
                <p>While the Liberian Government will be interested in the views of Lewis, it is
                    believed that he will have little actual authority.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hall</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d442-f1" n="34" xml:id="d442-f1-content">
                <p>He had arrived in Monrovia on June 14, 1936.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
